              {
                "id": "p90-q49-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cancel the common factor while retaining both domain exclusions; the cancelled factor produces the hole.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x-2)(x+1)}\\\\f(x)&=\\frac{x+2}{x-2}\\qquad(x\\ne-1,\\ x\\ne2).\\end{align*}$$\nCancelling $x+1$ does not restore the excluded input $x=-1$.\n\nFor the $x$-intercept,\n$$\\begin{align*}x+2&=0\\\\x&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe $x$-intercept is $(-2,0)$.\n\nFor the $y$-intercept,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(0)&=\\frac{2}{-2}\\\\f(0)&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe $y$-intercept is $(0,-1)$.\n\nThe remaining denominator gives the vertical asymptote $x=2$. The ratio of the leading coefficients gives the horizontal asymptote $y=1$.\n\nUse the reduced expression to locate the missing point:\n$$\\begin{align*}y_{\\text{hole}}&=\\frac{-1+2}{-1-2}\\\\y_{\\text{hole}}&=-\\frac13.\\end{align*}$$\nMark an open circle at $\\left(-1,-\\frac13\\right)$ because the original function remains undefined at $x=-1$. The branches lie above and right, and below and left, of $(2,1)$.",
